    "waiver1115pending": {
      "pending": true,
      "count": 1,
      "name": "Substance Use Disorder Care Initiative",
      "details": "Target Population: \"Nutrition supports: Individuals who are food insecure and meet clinical criteria (chronic health condition, behavioral health condition, pregnant or postpartum)\"\n\"Objective 5: Establish HRSN benefits, including Food is Medicine and Recuperative Care HRSN Pilot 5a: Proposed Food is Medicine Request Maine is requesting expenditure authority for federal matching funds to start Food is Medicine pilot programs. Pilots may include the following: food pharmacy/fruit and vegetable prescriptions, nutrition counseling/education, case management for nutrition benefits, and medically tailored meals. Background Food is Medicine (FIM) refers to a range of programs, services, and interventions that address the interconnection between nutrition and health outcomes, encompassing areas such as food access and tailored food assistance (e.g., medically tailored meals, vouchers for produce, etc.). Poor nutrition is the leading cause of chronic illness and death across the entire U.S. Food insecurity, characterized by limited or uncertain access to adequate food, is strongly linked to various negative health outcomes. These include increased risks of chronic diseases like diabetes, heart disease, and hypertension, as well as poor mental health, impaired physical development in children, and higher rates of hospitalization.34 A variety of independent FIM-aligned programs are offered in Maine through public and private agencies. However, in isolation, these services do not provide the full scope of FIM programming. Massachusetts' 1115 waiver supported FIM initiatives demonstrated significant improvements in health outcomes, leading to a reduction in hospitalizations and emergency department utilization.35 Eligibility Maine aims to implement the Food is Medicine initiatives through pilot projects. Eligible individuals include those enrolled in MaineCare, who are identified as being food insecure and meet one of the following criteria: • Have a chronic health condition such as diabetes, cancer, HIV, etc. • Have a behavioral health condition (defined as behaviors that affect mental and physical well-being, encompassing mental health and SUD). • Pregnant and postpartum individuals up to 6 months postpartum. Benefits • Case Management services to access food/nutrition services, such as: o Outreach and education o Linkage to other state and federal benefit programs (application assistance) • Nutrition counseling and instruction tailored to health risk or nutrition-sensitive health conditions, such as: o Guidance on selecting healthy food o Healthy meal preparation • Home-delivered meals or pantry stocking, tailored to health risk and eligibility criteria, or certain nutrition-sensitive health conditions and/or specifically for children or pregnant individuals (includes medically tailored meals) – up to 3 meals/day for up to 6 months • Nutrition prescriptions tailored to health risks or certain nutrition-sensitive conditions – up to 3 meals/day for up to 6 months o Fruit and Veggie Rx. o Protein boxes o Food pharmacies o Healthy food vouchers • Grocery provisions for high-risk individuals to avoid unnecessary admissions or institutionalization – up to 3 meals/day for up to 6 months\"\nmore info here: https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid/section-1115-demonstrations/downloads/mn-sud-pa-07102025.pdf",
      "submitted": "2025-01-09"
    },
    "waiver1915": {
      "approved": true,
      "count": 1,
      "name": "ME Elderly and Adults with Disabilities Waiver (0276.R06.00)",
      "details": "Provides care coordination, personal care, respite, financial management services, skills training, assistive technology, attendant care services, environmental modifications, home delivered meals, home health services, living well for better health (chronic disease self-management), matter of balance (falls prevention), non-medical transportation, and personal emergency response system services to individuals ages 65 or older and individuals with physical disabilities ages 18 or older who meet a hospital or nursing facility level of care.  This waiver operates with a concurrent 1915(b)(4) authority.\n\"In order to receive services under this waiver the Participant must be authorized at the time of assessment to receive at least one Section 19 waiver service. Once found eligible, Participants may access the following services, as available, under this waiver: Care Coordination, Home Health Services, Personal Care, Attendant Care Services, Respite Care (in home and institutional), Personal Emergency Response Systems, Assistive Technology, Home Delivered Meals, Living Well for Better Health, Matter of Balance, and Environmental Modifications as well as Skills Training, and Financial Management Services (FMS) for individuals who choose to self-direct Personal Care or in-home Respite Services.\" \"Service Definition (Scope): Home Delivered Meals are meals that are either hot, cold, shelf stable, or frozen meals, and that are delivered to the Participant's home. In order to be eligible, the Participant cannot reside in an institution that meets the definition of a hospital or nursing facility, and the Participant is unable to prepare their own meals and no one else is responsible to prepare the meals. Specify applicable (if any) limits on the amount, frequency, or duration of this service: Two meal per day and up to seven days per week.\" estimated at $9.11-$10.55 per meal",
      "dates": "Approval: 08/09/2023\nEffective: 07/01/2023\nExpiration: 06/30/2028"
    }
